<p>Oops I forgot I had placeholdered this yesterday until I went to bed but I have uh just managed to not go over 24hrs too much</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm a little conflicted on this as a whole: the final few paragraphs have this really delightful rising sense of dread that works for me in a really dark way that I really dig; but it feels&#8230;like the ending just kinda&#8230;happens? I was originally going to highlight this just appearing in wombs as being an issue:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX may manifest in the wombs of women who have undergone menopause and are the parent of adult children who currently identify as transgender or non-binary</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But I think it could be worked better into the ending: it feels, like you'd have this great opportunity for like&#8230;the SCP-XXXX instance to crumble into dust and emit more parasites and have like, the -1 instances really like&#8230;come together to crow about how good a job they did protecting their kid from being trans and like, regretting that being trans killed their kid while the narrative here is very framed around the mothers being more responsible?</p>
<p>It's a hard balance: in some ways, the ending resulting in more spread of this feels like it'd be more narratively satisfying but it also feels like it might really impact the kinda&#8230;broad impact that it currently has; where like, at the moment it affects basically every trans person. But I feel like the specific horror might outweigh that: in that, having it feel like a kinda localised, spreading phenomenon would help make this feel more <em>real</em> and hence more impactful?</p>
<p>I'm also kinda iffy about this starting in the mother at all as a thing; it feels&#8230;underutilised? Like a lot of the horror comes more from peoples reactions to this happening to -2, and so it feels like the instances originally being from the mother feels almost tangential?</p>
<p>There's some pacing issues: it kinda feels like the start of the description drags stuff out a lot in a very dry way; and it feels more concisely moving to the actual meat of this would land better.</p>
<p>There's a small part of me that feels like this needs more than a light touch up at the end: I can see like, a longer form diary or series of interviews say having a much larger effect here; but it feels like they might really struggle to show anything new that the description already implies, and I don't necessarily think having a big focus on a specific mother/child relation here would actually be the best way to do this.</p>
<hr />
<p>Speaking here from a sensitivity perspective alone: I don't think this has issues with that specifically; though there's definitely points where it feels like this is going to be really bad in that regard. I think tying the detransitioning stuff into people's reactions, and in turn, the eventual death into exclusively people being unsupportive works. I&#8230;am hesitant only in that this feels&#8230;miserable? It's very much a piece about despair and the loss of hope and while I personally think that it's done in a way which works, I can see people disagreeing and I would suggest reaching out to a few other trans/non-binary critters about this.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>Gonna note that I can miss stuff; flag things which are right as being wrong and indeed flag things which are deliberately wrong.</em><br />
<em>I'll likely use <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">strikethrough</span> to indicate stuff to remove and <span style="color: green">green text</span> to indicate stuff to add.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>openly transgender and non-binary individuals for signs of SCP-XXXX activity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm, not necessarily a fan of 'signs of SCP-XXXX activity' here: on the one hand, keeping it open for any kind of sign like this is probably good for ConProcs; but it feels like this is a really great moment for you to imply something specific to hook a reader in; perhaps 'for signs of parasitic infection by SCP-XXXX' or perhaps something more specific.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is then to be surgically removed from SCP-XXXX-2 and stored in isopropyl.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like either &quot;The SCP-XXXX instance&#8230;&quot; or &quot;All the SCP-XXXX instances&#8230;&quot; would work better here; really emphasise that there's not just one SCP but a whole host of them, y'know?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>is not conducive to healthy function</p>
</blockquote>
<p>something about 'healthy function' feels really odd here; it feels weirdly dehumanising and I'm not sure whether that's good or not?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to global and regional disparities in access to and quality of healthcare for trans and non-binary people, as well as the social capability for such individuals to be open about their identity, detection of SCP-XXXX activity is difficult.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Part of me wonders whether explicitly mentioning webcrawlers and social media here would be a good idea but I think this is fine?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>including a long prehensile tail and <span style="color: green">a</span> segmented anatomic structure.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is equipped with a jawless, toothed mouth and four hooklike claws on its underdeveloped appendages. Instances have been recorded to reach lengths greater than 0.4 meters.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I can't quite put my finger on why; but something about this flow feels iffy? I wonder whether moving the 'greater than 0.4meters' to a footnote after 'enlarged' would help?</p>
<p>It's possibly that this is the first time you mention 'instances' or the repetition of sentences starting with 'SCP-XXXX' being broken this way is a little jarring but I'm not sure.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Analysis has shown SCP-XXXX to be genetically identical to humans.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels&#8230;iffy; 'humans' feels like an overly broad thing for 'genetically identical' to work since like two humans can be relatively genetically different? I wonder whether specificity would help here: are they genetically identical to their host? To one specific human?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These subjects, hereby referred to as SCP-XXXX-1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It feels odd to have a -1 but not have anything in the ConProcs relating to it</p>
<blockquote>
<p>is sleeping. it then makes its way to offspring of SCP-XXXX-1 in proximity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>missing capital; and ending on 'in proximity' feels like it's missing a noun (like, in that, it's implied you mean 'in proximity to -1' but the construction feels iffy cos there's no explicit thing saying that); and also feels vague; perhaps tying the 'travel up to a kilometer' line in here would work better instead of having it in the next sentence?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX will attach itself to offspring of SCP-XXXX-1 that identify as transgender or non-binary</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'identify' feels odd here: it kinda implies that a) people who aren't trans but identify as can be affected and b) people who are trans but don't identify as such can't be affected and I wonder whether just saying 'offspring of -1 that are' would be better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX does not feed on SCP-XXXX-2 directly.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels a little weirdly placed right now: it's kinda mentioned in passing in the middle of a paragraph, but the idea of feeding isn't touched on more for like&#8230;two, large paragraphs more, and so it feels like perhaps it'd work best just before the &quot;SCP-XXXX is thought to feed on positive attention paid to&#8230;.&quot; sentence?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, the perceived appearance of SCP-XXXX-2 will differ from their actual pre-exposure appearance in multiple ways. These inconsistencies mimic the secondary sexual characteristics of the sex assigned to SCP-XXXX-2 at birth, as well as non-genetic traits stereotypically associated with that sex by the culture of the subject observing SCP-XXXX-2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This section, and the rest of the paragraph it's in, feels&#8230;like it uses a lot of words to not say a lot: the dense, clinical tone feels like it doesn't setup a very evocative image of the transformation. I feel like perhaps trimming down the list of alterations a little, and perhaps mentioning a few examples might convey this more evocatively but it might be hard to get the examples to feel right here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If SCP-XXXX-2 possesses a chosen name different from the name given to them at birth, they will become unable to pronounce it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Does this apply if they meet someone else using the name? I guess this isn't fully a critique so much as me wondering whether this has much larger scale impacts than intended?</p>
</div>
