<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Follow&nbsp;up&nbsp;questions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">wow&nbsp;you&nbsp;opened&nbsp;the&nbsp;box</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>but it feels…like the ending just kinda…happens?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's important to me for multiple reasons that whatever other changes are made, The nature of the final paragraph remains. I find the image of a trans person physically slicing the embodiment of transphobia from themselves to be very powerful, and the final line is basically the closest this thing can have to a happy ending. I'm wondering, do you have any suggestions for making that final paragraph more impactful or obviously important to the metaphor? I don't want readers to gloss over it by taking it too literally.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it feels, like you'd have this great opportunity for like…the SCP-XXXX instance to crumble into dust and emit more parasites</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I did consider this, but decided against it as really, these aren't parasites as we understand them but are rather the embodiment of the gender that people feel a trans person <em>should</em> have. Therefore, their ultimate purpose is to totally usurp the host like a cuckoo bird, not to propagate.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>like, the -1 instances really like…come together to crow about how good a job they did protecting their kid from being trans and like, regretting that being trans killed their kid</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a good idea, but I can't think of a way to do it that isn't too heavy handed or takes away focus from the ending. Further advice would be appreciated.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm also kinda iffy about this starting in the mother at all as a thing; it feels…underutilised?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is very fair. Originally the piece was going to be <em>all</em> about mothers, but as the scope of the metaphor expanded it fell by the wayside. I'm not sure how to incorporate it or trim it, although it seems important for thematic purposes that it remain in some form.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I…am hesitant only in that this feels…miserable?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I am very interested to hear some thoughts about how I could intensify the glimmers of hope that I want to end the piece with.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Thanks for some great crit.</p>

							</div>

								
					
