<p><em>drops in</em> time 4 thoughts</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;smells&nbsp;like&nbsp;brain</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;thonk</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Preface: I <em>am</em> a trans person, but it goes without saying that my own thoughts don't stand for the whole, and I'd still recommend seeking out more willing trans/nb critters.</p>
<p>Okay SO, I read the first(?) iteration of this originally about a week+ back, and I can definitely say that some of my original opinions have changed for this article.</p>
<p>At first - and this was very much a more personal gripe, mind you - I really wasn't&#8230;<em>feeling</em> it, y'know? It was a pretty solid draft but initially it wasn't necessarily to my tastes.</p>
<p>This is no longer much the case, though.</p>
<p>The addition of the diary excerpts definitely makes this, for me - it's a perfect touch of a human element I didn't realise that the original iteration was lacking. The slow creep of dread and anguish that you build up through it is, truth be told, <em>viscerally</em> uncomfortable but in the way that's reflective of good, solid writing - as a transmasc person <em>specifically</em> I felt that shit in my soul.<br />
(Moreover the fact Leif was okay and this wasn't gonna end up being a more tragic example of the anomaly's effect was a <em>huge</em> relief tbh)</p>
<p>I still do hold this worry about this anomalisation of the concept of dysphoria, in the context of trans and nb people, though perhaps a little less now. Moreover I did not think my stance on that would change, but lo, here we are.</p>
<p>I don't think I really have much negative to say beyond the personal concerns I've laid out abane this paragraph. You took a solid working draft and you made it into a <em>good</em> draft, in my opinion, capturing perfectly the reality and horror of what it's like to deal with dysphoria. Furthermore, it doesn't feel like the article is <em>forcing</em> the idea that dysphoria has to be to particular severity.</p>
<p>I look forward to seeing where this goes. It certainly is interesting.</p>
</div>
